# Mathematics still to be completed
#
# Chapters 1-3 — foundational linear algebra
# The remaining gaps are the selected complex and vector-space structures,
# finite-list exchange and basis theorems, matrix/rank constructions, quotient
# well-definedness, inverse selection, and dual-space constructions. The
# source-facing definitions and results are present, but these constructions
# and proofs have not all been supplied.
#
# Chapter 4 — polynomial factorization
# The coordinate complex-number laws, polynomial representation and division,
# and the analytic minimum-modulus argument used by the fundamental theorem of
# algebra still need complete proofs.
#
# Chapter 5, Sections 5A-5E — operators
# The finite-sum algebra for polynomial evaluation, minimal-polynomial
# existence and consequences, invariant restrictions, triangularization,
# diagonalization, eigenspace dimension arguments, Gershgorin's theorem, and
# simultaneous-basis results still contain unproved mathematical steps.
#
# Chapter 6, Sections 6A-6C — inner-product spaces
# The scalar-geometry instances, norm inequalities, Gram-Schmidt and Riesz
# constructions, orthogonal decompositions and projections, and pseudoinverse
# results retain incomplete existence or proof steps.
#
# Chapter 7, Section 7A — Results 7.5-7.23
# The adjoint construction is uniquely characterized, and Result 7.4 is
# checked. The adjoint algebra, null/range identities, self-adjoint criteria,
# and normal-operator consequences still need their full proofs.
#
# Chapter 7, Section 7B — Results 7.26, 7.27, 7.29, and 7.31
# The four source statements and the concrete calculations in Examples 7.30
# and 7.33 are present. The quadratic positivity proof, real factor elimination, and the
# real and complex spectral-theorem arguments remain to be completed.
#
# Chapter 7, Section 7C — Results 7.38, 7.39, and 7.43
# Positivity, square-root candidacy, the six-way characterization, exact unique
# existence, callable square-root notation, and the three checked source
# examples are present. The spectral construction and uniqueness proof remain,
# as does the two-move proof of 7.43. The callable positive-square-root selector
# is one explicit trust boundary because subtype-valued selection is not yet
# stable in an importing caller.
#
# Chapter 7, Section 7D — Results 7.49, 7.53, 7.55, 7.57, 7.58, 7.61, 7.63
# The four source definitions, all eight results, and the three explanatory
# examples are present. Result 7.54 has a checked source-length proof. The two
# characterization cycles, the complex spectral description, QR, the strict
# positivity characterization, and Cholesky retain seven named axiom
# boundaries. No direct trust was added in this section.
#
# A direct importing caller of the coordinate matrix helpers can expose the
# existing nested-template binding failure in chap3b::scalar_finite_sum. That
# verifier issue is tracked as kernel_problem in the working todo rather than
# being hidden as mathematical proof debt.
#
# Chapter 7, Section 7E — Results 7.64-7.80
# The ordered singular-value interface, its multiplicities, the operator and
# matrix SVD relations, and all three source examples are present. Spectral
# ordering, SVD construction, adjoint/pseudoinverse formulas, and the matrix
# coordinate transport remain to be proved. Selecting the dependent-length
# ordered list is one explicit trust boundary after exact unique existence.
#
# Chapter 7, Section 7F — Results 7.82-7.111
# Operator norm, low-rank truncation, polar decomposition, and all geometric
# set-valued constructions are present, together with five formal examples.
# The SVD norm estimates, best-approximation lower bound, polar factors, image
# equalities, and volume scaling remain. General volume is visibly trusted at
# the analytic boundary explicitly deferred by the source.
#
# Chapter 8, Sections 8A-8D — complexification, generalized eigenspaces,
# nilpotent operators, and trace
# Every retained definition and result is present. The remaining work is the
# complexification transport, primary-decomposition and Jordan-chain
# constructions, nilpotent block bases, trace basis independence, and the
# eigenvalue/coefficient/inner-product trace formulas. The selected
# generalized-eigenvalue list and operator trace remain explicit trust
# boundaries after their source relations are stated.
#
# Chapter 9, Section 9A — bilinear and quadratic forms
# Bilinear, symmetric, alternating, and quadratic-form carriers are callable,
# and all retained matrix, decomposition, characterization, and
# diagonalization results are present. Coordinate reconstruction,
# polarization, and diagonal-basis constructions remain named proof debt.
#
# Chapter 9, Section 9B — alternating multilinear forms
# Slot replacement, permutations, inversion sign, signed coordinate sums, and
# full alternating-form spaces are explicit. Finite permutation enumeration,
# swap parity, multilinear expansion, and the one-dimensionality argument
# remain trust boundaries.
#
# Chapter 9, Section 9C — determinants
# The basis-free operator determinant, matrix determinant, both characteristic
# polynomial routes, evaluation rules, and geometric applications are all
# present. Their remaining proofs depend on full alternating-form expansion,
# triangularization, spectral transport, QR, and finite polynomial identities.
#
# Chapter 9, Section 9D — tensor products
# Two-space and finite-family tensor products follow the source's
# multilinear-functional-on-duals construction. Pure tensors, dimensions,
# bases, inner products, and both universal-map directions are callable. The
# dependent component/dual structures, tensor-basis arguments, and universal
# extension proofs remain explicit trust boundaries.
